prompt:
You are a sceptical financial analyst reviewing a forecast prepared by a colleague.

Given the following forecast for LT (base year 2022):
{
  "strategy": "baseline",
  "base_year": 2022,
  "forecast": [
    {
      "year": 2023,
      "revenue_cr": 183939,
      "net_income_cr": 10192,
      "eps": 72.54
    },
    {
      "year": 2024,
      "revenue_cr": 216120,
      "net_income_cr": 11976,
      "eps": 85.24
    },
    {
      "year": 2025,
      "revenue_cr": 253965,
      "net_income_cr": 14072,
      "eps": 100.16
    }
  ],
  "reasoning": "From the 10-year highlights table, Gross Revenue from Operations (consolidated) was \u20b9135,979 crore in FY2021 and \u20b9156,521 crore in FY2022, implying a revenue YoY growth rate of approximately 15.1%. For Net Income (PAT attributable to Group shareholders including exceptional items), FY2022 was \u20b98,669 crore vs FY2021 \u20b911,583 crore; however, FY2021 was inflated by one-time divestment gains from the E&A business sale, so instead the underlying PAT excluding exceptional items grew from \u20b96,965 crore to \u20b98,572 crore (~23.1% growth), which is used as the net income growth rate. Shares outstanding at end of FY2022 are approximately 140.50 crore (face value \u20b92 each), used as the denominator for EPS calculation across all forecast years."
}

Step 1: Identify the 3 most critical assumptions embedded in this forecast.
Step 2: For each assumption, describe one plausible scenario where it fails.
Step 3: Rate each assumption failure as Low / Medium / High risk to the forecast accuracy.
Step 4: Assign an overall confidence score from 0-100 (100 = completely certain).

RESPONSE FORMAT: Begin your response DIRECTLY with the opening brace { — no reasoning or text before the JSON.

Return ONLY valid JSON:
{
  "company": "<COMPANY>",
  "base_year": <YYYY>,
  "strategy": "<baseline|alt_strategy>",
  "assumptions": [
    {"assumption": "<text>", "failure_scenario": "<text>", "risk": "<Low|Medium|High>"}
  ],
  "confidence_score": <0-100>,
  "overall_critique": "<2 sentences>"
}

response:
```json
{
  "company": "LT (Larsen & Toubro)",
  "base_year": 2022,
  "strategy": "baseline",
  "assumptions": [
    {
      "assumption": "Revenue grows at a constant 15.1% YoY, anchored solely on one year of historical growth (FY2021 to FY2022), with no mean-reversion or cyclical adjustment.",
      "failure_scenario": "A broad macroeconomic slowdown, tightening of government capital expenditure budgets, or a sharp rise in input costs compresses order inflows and execution rates, causing revenue growth to decelerate to 7-9% — roughly half the assumed rate — by FY2024-25, making the FY2025 revenue projection overstated by ~₹50,000 crore.",
      "risk": "High"
    },
    {
      "assumption": "Net income grows at 23.1% per annum, derived from the underlying PAT excluding exceptional items, implying sustained or improving net profit margins (~5.5% rising toward ~5.8%) over the forecast horizon.",
      "failure_scenario": "Rising subcontractor costs, commodity price inflation (steel, cement), wage escalation, and increased competition on large infrastructure bids compress EBITDA margins by 100-150 bps. Combined with higher finance costs from increased working capital borrowings on a larger order book, net margin remains flat or contracts, capping net income growth well below 23% and rendering FY2025 PAT estimates overstated by 20-30%.",
      "risk": "High"
    },
    {
      "assumption": "Shares outstanding remain fixed at 140.50 crore across all forecast years, implying no equity dilution from ESOPs, QIPs, convertible instruments, or buybacks.",
      "failure_scenario": "LT executes a fresh equity raise or a large ESOP tranche vesting to fund overseas acquisitions or technology investments, increasing the diluted share count by 2-4%, which mechanically reduces EPS below forecasted levels even if net income hits targets — a modest but systematically ignored drag on per-share metrics.",
      "risk": "Low"
    }
  ],
  "confidence_score": 38,
  "overall_critique": "The forecast relies on a single data point for both revenue and profit growth rates, extrapolating a potentially peak-cycle or post-COVID rebound year into a straight-line three-year trajectory, which materially overstates the reliability of the projection. The use of adjusted PAT (ex-exceptionals) as the growth anchor is directionally sensible but the 23.1% net income CAGR assumption is aggressive and unsupported by any margin analysis, competitive landscape assessment, or order book conversion rate scrutiny, making the EPS estimates particularly vulnerable to downside."
}
```

